## Highlights
- DAGs are visual representations of qualitative causal assumptions: They encode researchers’ expert knowledge and beliefs about how the world works. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j7wh5sbzr3bbyba2vhgby3c8))
    - Note: DAGs are pictures that show how people think different things are connected and affect each other. They represent what researchers believe about how things happen in the world.
- *Identification analysis* determines whether, and under which conditions, it is possible to strip an observed association of all its spurious components. We say that a causal effect is *identified* if a properly stripped association equals (“identifies”) the causal effect. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j7whav3nqszzpf0gkd9yrtv3))
- Identification analysis requires causal assumptions about how the data were generated. The sum of these causal assumptions is called a *causal model*, which must describe both how the world works (how observed and unobserved variables take their values) and how the data were collected (what variables and variable values are recorded) (Greenland 2010). ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j7whcasrzs0cb2wmdhgzha45))
- Identification is not the same as estimation. Identification refers to the *possibility* of correctly estimating a causal effect asymptotically from a given set of observed variables, as the number of observations goes to infinity. ([View Highlight](https://read.readwise.io/read/01j7whf8y4zt014frh5r2s2t6y))
